  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "9eXWGZSkUsIquYDE9C+w9NV+Qkw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:42.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "A Common Data Environment (CDE) is used for sharing 3D models and directly associated data with all stakeholders. What lives there is the **federated model**: discipline-specific part models \u2014 structural engineering (load-bearing design), MEPF (mechanical, electricity, plumbing, fire safety), ventilation/HVAC \u2014 broken up as required by the BEP and combined in the CDE for **collision detection and management**, a core BIM activity (01:13:06). Deep collision analysis is typically done by architects and engineers, not BIM management (01:14:28).\n\nThe platform landscape (BIM briefing + DT BAU walkthrough):\n- **Autodesk Construction Cloud (ACC)**, now the Autodesk Forma family\n- **Dalux** \u2014 Danish, closed-source, limited API; explicitly called out as difficult to integrate because it is a closed system (00:07:31)\n- **Catenda** \u2014 open API but lacking some richer features\n- **Squirrel** \u2014 another entrant\n\nOpenness is the integration bottleneck: closed CDEs restrict custom agents and software (02:05:54), which is precisely the gap a local-first, integrable platform can exploit. Data sovereignty adds another axis: tools can be prohibited when technology or hosting is not EU-based \u2014 e.g. Autodesk Forma on some projects (01:38:41).",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T18:11:42.083125+00:00"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:42.000Z"
    }
  },
